(  # Report) Tj
T*
(  Yihao Yin \(yihaoyin\)) Tj
T*
(  ) Tj
T*
(  ## Part 1 The Three Clocks) Tj
T*
(  ### Results) Tj
T*
(  | messages | tags | timestamp minus sourceTimestamp \(plant s\) |) Tj
T*
(  |---|---|---:|) Tj
T*
(  | live \(445\) | 34 continuous | 0 |) Tj
T*
(  | live | 14 analysers, every 0.1 h | 0 or 180 |) Tj
T*
(  | live | 5 analysers, every 0.25 h | 0 to 720 |) Tj
T*
(  | replayed, isHistorical \(129\) | all 53 | 1620 to 7200, median 3960 |) Tj
T*
(  ### Discussion) Tj
T*
(  Windows are keyed on sourceTimestamp, the event time, because it is the only clock that says when the plant) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(was in the state a value describes; the message timestamp says when a batch was assembled and) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(receivedAt when this laptop saw it. 402 of the 443 message timestamps carry more than one event time.) Tj
T*
(  The largest gap, 7200 plant s, is on a replayed message: the link dropped four times, for 32 to 46 wall) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(seconds \(77 to 111 plant minutes\), and each backlog was resent stamped with the time of resending. On) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(live messages the gap comes from the analysers, which republish an analysis unchanged until the next) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(one, so the lag reaches analyserIntervalHours x 3600 - 180, 720 s for the 0.25 h analysers.) Tj
T*
(  receivedAt is on another machine: converted to plant time it lands a median 31 plant s before the live) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(timestamp, so this laptop runs about 0.2 wall s behind the publisher.) Tj
T*
(  ) Tj
T*
